{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,135p' test/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc && sed -n '1,120p' src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/publication.cljc && sed -n '2030,2067p' src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc && sed -n '2185,2310p' src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,135p' test/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc && sed -n '1,120p' src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/publication.cljc && sed -n '2030,2067p' src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc && sed -n '2185,2310p' src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"(ns yang.python.antlr.linked-harness\n  \"The linked profile's composition for the Python tests (C4 slices P2\n   and P3, docs/design/yang.antlr.md 8.5.6): the namespace that requires\n   this is the publisher of `py` and `pysp`, so it publishes each module\n   once per process into one in-memory content store and serves them to\n   every linked run over a `yin.vm.linker.interpreter` composition under\n   `:trusted` derivation (the remediation ruling's R6). The `:verifying`\n   evidence is `publish-module!`'s own `link-local` links, which\n   `linked-prelude-test` asserts.\n\n   A linked run builds the program's task on one of the three vector VMs\n   with a link pair, then runs the VM and serves the pair alternately\n   until the VM stops blocking, as the REPL's drive does. The AST walker\n   is not a backend here: its link of `py` is refused `:undeclared-free`\n   until L-b.\"\n  (:require [yin.vm.semantic.linearize :as sr-linearize]\n            [yin.vm.semantic.derive :as derive]\n            [dao.jing.mem :as mem]\n            [yang.core.call :as call]\n            [yang.python.antlr.prelude :as prelude]\n            [yang.python.antlr.safepoint :as safepoint]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.data :as data]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn-code :as dcode]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn-register-code :as rcode]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn.register :as rvm]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn.stack :as dvm]\n            [yin.vm.integer :as integer]\n            [yin.vm.linker.interpreter :as link]\n            [yin.vm.linker.publish :as publish]\n            [yin.vm.module :as module]\n            [yin.vm.semantic :as semantic]\n            [yin.vm.test-utils :as tu]))\n\n\n(def integer-limits\n  \"The ordinary Python composition's `integer` limits.\"\n  {::integer/max-bits 100000, ::integer/max-digits 4300})\n\n\n(defn registry\n  \"The full host registry a linked Python composition registers: the\n   `require` handler, then `cell`, `data`, `integer` under `limits`, and\n   `stream` — the hook module `pysp` polls a signal stream, so the\n   safepointed composition needs it even though `py` does not.\"\n  ([] (registry integer-limits))\n  ([limits]\n   (-> (module/default-registry)\n       module/register-cell-module\n       (data/register-data-module {::data/max-items 1048576})\n       (prelude/register-integer-module limits)\n       module/register-stream-module)))\n\n\n(def published\n  \"`yang.call` and `py` published once into their own content store.\"\n  (delay\n    (let [store (mem/create-content-mem)\n          call-result (publish/publish-module! store (call/module-spec (registry)))\n          result (publish/publish-module! store\n                                          (prelude/module-spec (registry)\n                                                               (:address call-result)))]\n      {:store store, :result result, :address (:address result),\n       :call-result call-result, :call-address (:address call-result)})))\n\n\n(def published-pysp\n  \"`pysp` published once into `py`'s own content store, which its pinned\n   requirement already lives in: the same shape as `published`.\"\n  (delay\n    (let [{:keys [store address]} @published\n          result (publish/publish-module!\n                   store (safepoint/module-spec (registry) address))]\n      {:store store, :result result, :address (:address result)})))\n\n\n(defn source\n  \"The serving composition over the published store: `name-env` defaults\n   to `{py <address> yang.call <address>}`, derivation `:trusted`, the test being the\n   publisher. `pysp`-serving sources pass their own `name-env`, the\n   safepointed runs' `{'py a :pysp b}` both.\"\n  ([] (source {'py (:address @published),\n               'yang.call (:call-address @published)}))\n  ([name-env]\n   (link/composition {:content-store (:store @published),\n                      :name-env name-env,\n                      :derivation :trusted})))\n\n\n(defn safepoint-source\n  \"The serving composition for `pysp`, `py`, and the `yang.call` module\n   required by `py`.\"\n  []\n  (source {'py (:address @published), 'pysp (:address @published-pysp),\n           'yang.call (:call-address @published)}))\n\n\n(defn publish-guest-module!\n  \"One guest module's `spec` (lower/module-spec's) published into `py`'s\n   own content store, where its pinned requirements already live:\n   `publish-module!`'s answer. Guest modules are tiny beside `py`, so a\n   test may publish its own; publish imports bottom-up, the `deps` map\n   naming each import's address.\"\n  [spec]\n  (publish/publish-module! (:store @published) spec))\n\n\n(def backends\n  \"Each vector backend's root task over a program AST and composition.\"\n  {:semantic (fn [ast opts]\n               (semantic/load-ast (semantic/create-vm opts)\n                                  (vm/ast->datoms ast)\n                                  vm/ast-contract)),\n   :stack (fn [ast opts]\n            (dvm/create-vm (:image (derive/stack-image (:vector (sr-linearize/project ast))))\n                           (assoc opts :contract vm/stack-contract))),\n   :register (fn [ast opts]\n               (rvm/create-vm (derive/register-image (:vector (sr-linearize/project ast)))\n                              (assoc opts :contract vm/register-contract)))})\n\n\n(def continues\n  \"Each vector backend's admission of the next top-level unit to a halted\n   task, as `yin.repl` admits an input line: the task keeps its store,\n   heap, module registry and module stores.\"\n  {:semantic (fn [task ast]\n               (semantic/load-ast task (vm/ast->datoms ast) vm/ast-contract)),\n   :stack (fn [task ast]\n            (let [img (:image (derive/stack-image (:vector (sr-linearize/project ast))))\n                  attached (dvm/attach-image task img vm/stack-contract)]\n              (assoc attached\n                     :pc (dvm/absolute-pc attached [(dcode/image-hash img) 0])\n                     :frames [] :stack [] :continuation []\n                     :halted? false :blocked? false :value nil))),\n   :register (fn [task ast]\n(ns yang.python.antlr.publication\n  \"C4 slices I3 and I5 (docs/design/yang.antlr.md 8.5.6): a set of compiled Python\n   units published as linker modules. The ordering, SCC bundling and publish\n   loop are `yang.core.publication`'s; this namespace supplies the Python\n   hooks: the imports `lower/imported-module-names` hoists, the\n   `lower/module-spec` and `lower/scc-module-specs` emitters, the I7 site\n   set, and no binder table. Source modules that read `yang.call` pin the\n   address from the published `py` module's requirements.\n   Name assertions stay the linker's (`yin.vm.linker.publish/assertion`\n   and `name-envelopes`): this namespace publishes content, it signs\n   nothing.\"\n  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [yang.core.publication :as core]\n            [yang.python.antlr.lower :as lower]\n            [yang.python.antlr.siteset :as siteset]\n            [yin.vm.linker.publish :as publish]))\n\n\n(defn- unit-imports\n  \"The Python modules unit `u` statically imports.\"\n  [{:keys [name package? packet]}]\n  (lower/imported-module-names packet\n                               {:name name, :package? (boolean package?)}))\n\n\n(defn- config\n  [publish-fn call-address]\n  {:imported-names unit-imports,\n   :module-spec (fn [registry {:keys [name package? packet]} py-address deps]\n                  (lower/module-spec registry packet\n                                     {:name name, :package? package?,\n                                      :py-address py-address, :call-address call-address,\n                                      :deps deps})),\n   :scc-module-specs (fn [registry units py-address deps]\n                       (lower/scc-module-specs registry units py-address call-address deps)),\n   :site-set siteset/publish!,\n   :binder-table nil,\n   :refusal-ns \"yang.python.antlr\",\n   :publish-fn publish-fn})\n\n\n(defn publication-order\n  \"Dependency-first names, retaining the I3 order for acyclic units.\n   Cyclic components appear as sorted member vectors in :order, one\n   publication step per SCC. External imports must be in `published`.\"\n  [units published]\n  (core/publication-order (config publish/publish-module! nil) units published))\n\n\n(defn publish-units!\n  \"Publish `units` (`[{:name :package? :packet}]`) into the content store\n   `handle` over the host registry `registry`, `py` pinned at\n   `py-address` and each import outside the set at its address in\n   `published` ({python-name address}), in `publication-order`. Answers\n   `{:status :ok :addresses {python-name address}\n     :bundles {bundle-symbol address}\n     :sites {python-name-or-bundle-symbol site-publication}}` for the units\n   of the set (`siteset/publish!`'s answer for each module published beside\n   its code image, I7; an SCC member alias carries no marks and so no\n   entry, its bundle does). The publisher that asserts these names\n   indexes `siteset/signed-entries` of `:sites` in the same index\n   transaction; a module whose entry is missing is refused by every\n   hook-profile reader. Or the\n   first refusal: `publication-order`'s before anything is written, else\n   `publish-module!`'s with a string `:unit`: the Python name for a member,\n   or the bundle module name for an SCC bundle. Every bundle name must be\n   asserted/bound alongside the member names for aliases to deliver.\n   Optional `publish-fn` takes `[handle spec]`, defaulting to\n   `publish/publish-module!`, for both bundles and members.\"\n  ([handle registry py-address units]\n   (publish-units! handle registry py-address units {}))\n  ([handle registry py-address units published]\n   (publish-units! handle registry py-address units published publish/publish-module!))\n  ([handle registry py-address units published publish-fn]\n   (let [py-manifest (when (and handle py-address)\n                       (jing/get handle py-address nil))\n         call-address (get-in py-manifest [:yin.module/requires 'yang.call])]\n     (core/publish-units! (config publish-fn call-address)\n                          handle registry py-address units published))))\n(defn lower-packet\n  \"The complete program for one ok packet: the module body run by\n   `py/run-main`, with tail calls marked. Its value is `{:py/out [...]\n   :py/exception nil-or-{:type :args}}`. The packet may declare the\n   decimal digit budget of its literals as `:yang.python.antlr/max-digits`.\n\n   `:prelude` selects how the runtime profile arrives: `:bundled` (the\n   default) carries the prelude tree ahead of the body; `:linked`\n   requires `yang.call` and `py` from the task's link pair, then allocates\n   the task's runtime state with `(py/init!)`. Either way the body names only\n   `py/` exports, never a runtime key.\n\n   Guest imports deliver through try-require thunks at the executed\n   statement, after checking sys.modules. Only py is eagerly required;\n   an import-free unit's tree is unchanged. The program is __main__, a\n   packageless unit, so its relative imports raise. Imports need the linked\n   prelude -- a module closure's free reads never see the ambient store,\n   so a bundled importer's `py/*` definitions are invisible to an\n   imported body -- and a bundled unit holding any import statement but\n   a plain import of sys, builtins or importlib is refused. Literal\n   import_module calls that deliver guest modules need the linked profile.\"\n  ([packet] (lower-packet packet {}))\n  ([packet {:keys [prelude], :or {prelude :bundled}}]\n   (let [pk (p/validate! packet {:diagnostic-ns diagnostic-ns})\n         main (app* 'py/run-main\n                    (u/lam [globals-sym globals-fn-sym]\n                           (lower-module-body packet)))]\n     (u/mark-tails\n       (case prelude\n         :bundled (if-not (preseeded-imports-only? pk)\n                    (throw (ex-info\n                             \"Imports need the linked prelude (docs/design/yang.antlr.md 8.5.6)\"\n                             {:yang.python.antlr/diagnostic :yang.python.antlr/unsupported,\n                              :construct \"import under the bundled prelude\"}))\n                    (u/then prelude/uast main))\n         :linked (u/seq-nodes\n                   [(app* 'require (u/lit 'yang.call))\n                    (app* 'require (u/lit prelude/module-name))\n          (u/let1 '%from-name n\n                  (u/let1 '%from-result (app* 'cell/new none)\n                          (app* 'py/try\n                                (u/lam [] (app* 'cell/set! (u/v '%from-result)\n                                                (app* 'py/import-from (u/v '%from-module) (u/v '%from-name))))\n                                (u/lam ['%from-error]\n                                       (u/if-node\n                                         (app* 'get (app* 'cell/get (u/v '%from-module))\n                                               (u/lit initializing-key) (u/lit false))\n                                         (app* 'py/import-fail\n                                               (app* 'py/str\n                                                     (app* 'data/str-concat\n                                                           (u/lit \"cannot import name '\")\n                                                           (app* 'get (u/v '%from-name) (u/lit :py/str))\n                                                           (u/lit \"' from partially initialized module '\")\n                                                           (app* 'get (app* 'py/lookup-attr (u/v '%from-module)\n                                                                            (u/lit \"__name__\")) (u/lit :py/str))\n                                                           (u/lit \"' (most likely due to a circular import)\"))))\n                                         (app* 'py/raise (u/v '%from-error))))\n                                (u/lam [] (app* 'cell/get (u/v '%from-result))))))))\n\n\n(defn- scc-module-specs*\n  \"Canonical I5 construction: sorted members, indexed bare exports\n   m0-spec/m0-body, etc.; one pyscc.<mangled-members> bundle followed by\n   aliases in member order. Internal deliveries read those exports without\n   requiring aliases. The bundle pins py, yang.call when a body reads it,\n   and outgoing imports.\n   Alias :requires is filled with the bundle address after publication.\"\n  [registry units py-address call-address deps]\n  (doseq [{:keys [name]} units]\n    (validate-module-name! name))\n  (let [units (vec (sort-by :name units))\n        deps (apply dissoc deps (map :name units))\n        bundle-name (symbol (str \"pyscc.\" (str/join \"$$\" (map #(str/replace (:name %) \".\" \"$\") units))))\n        keys-for (into {} (map-indexed\n                            (fn [i unit]\n                              [(:name unit) [(symbol (str \"m\" i \"-spec\"))\n                                             (symbol (str \"m\" i \"-body\"))]]) units))\n        units-by-name (into {} (map (fn [u] [(:name u) u]) units))\n        internal-names (into {} (map (fn [nm] [(module-symbol nm) nm])) (keys keys-for))\n        internal (set (keys internal-names))\n        rewrite (fn [node]\n                  (cond\n                    (and (= :variable (:type node))\n                         (symbol? (:name node))\n                         (some? (namespace (:name node)))\n                         (contains? internal (symbol (namespace (:name node))))\n                         (= \"spec\" (name (:name node))))\n                    (let [nm (get internal-names (symbol (namespace (:name node))))\n                          u (get units-by-name nm)]\n                      (u/lit {:name nm, :package? (boolean (:package? u))}))\n                    (and (= :variable (:type node))\n                         (symbol? (:name node))\n                         (some? (namespace (:name node)))\n                         (contains? internal (symbol (namespace (:name node))))\n                         (= \"body\" (name (:name node))))\n                    (let [nm (get internal-names (symbol (namespace (:name node))))\n                          [_ body-key] (get keys-for nm)]\n                      (u/v body-key))\n                    (and (= :application (:type node))\n                         (= 'try-require (get-in node [:operator :name]))\n                         (contains? internal (get-in node [:operands 0 :value])))\n                    (u/lit {:status :ok})\n                    (and (= :application (:type node))\n                         (= 'py/import-from (get-in node [:operator :name])))\n                    (apply cyclic-import-from (:operands node))\n                    :else node))\n        wrapped-bodies\n        (mapv (fn [{:keys [name packet package?]}]\n                (let [unit {:name name, :package? (boolean package?)}\n                      body (walk/postwalk rewrite (lower-module-body packet unit))\n                      flag (fn [v]\n                             (app* 'cell/set! (u/v '%module)\n                                   (app* 'assoc (app* 'cell/get (u/v '%module))\n                                         (u/lit initializing-key) (u/lit v))))]\n                  (u/lam [globals-sym globals-fn-sym]\n                         (u/let1 '%module (app* 'py/modules-ref (u/lit {:py/str name}))\n                                 (u/then (flag true)\n                                         (app* 'py/try-finally (u/lam [] body)\n                                               (u/lam [u/discard] (flag false))))))))\n              units)\n        spec-defs\n        (mapcat (fn [{:keys [name package?]}]\n                  (let [[spec-key _] (get keys-for name)\n                        unit {:name name, :package? (boolean package?)}]\n                    [(u/def! spec-key (u/lit unit))]))\n                units)\n        body-defs\n        (map (fn [{:keys [name]} body]\n               (let [[_ body-key] (get keys-for name)]\n                 (u/def! body-key body)))\n             units wrapped-bodies)\n        ;; The linker discharges body reads only when their definitions\n        ;; precede every module-level application site. Install the pure\n        ;; sibling bindings before require; defining the lambdas runs no py.\n        needs-call? (some calls-yang-call? wrapped-bodies)\n        ast (module-tree (concat spec-defs\n                                 body-defs\n                                 (when needs-call? [(require-node 'yang.call)])\n                                 [(require-node prelude/module-name)]))\n        exports (set (mapcat val keys-for))\n        bundle (core-module/module-spec\n                 :yang.python.antlr/refusal :yang.python.antlr/undeclared-free\n                 registry\n                 {:name bundle-name,\n                  :ast ast,\n                  :exports exports,\n                  :runtime-name prelude/module-name,\n                  :runtime-address py-address,\n                  :deps (cond-> (into {} (map (fn [[n a]] [(module-symbol n) a])) deps)\n                          needs-call? (assoc 'yang.call call-address))})\n        require-profile (vm/profile-of vm/primitives 'require)]\n    (core-module/scc-module-specs\n      bundle\n      (mapv (fn [{:keys [name]}]\n              (let [[sk bk] (get keys-for name)]\n                (core-module/alias-spec (module-symbol name) module-exports\n                                        require-profile bundle-name sk bk)))\n            units))))\n\n\n(defn scc-module-specs\n  \"Canonical I5 construction: sorted members, indexed bare exports\n   m0-spec/m0-body, etc.; one pyscc.<mangled-members> bundle followed by\n   aliases in member order. Internal deliveries read those exports without\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"agent_message","text":"The reproduction confirms the `missing-site-set` is a separate failure mode: the refusal names the `yang.call` runtime module itself, before any Python module’s absent dependency can be exercised. That means the new unconditional Python dependency may expose a second publication gap: the `yang.call` module is published by the test harness without the site-set evidence required by its `:py/hooks` profile. I’m checking the call module’s profile and current publication helper before choosing a scoped fix."}}
